  "viva_qa": [
    {
      "q": "Why did you limit the circuit to 4 qubits and 4 PCA features?",
      "a": "Simulating noisy open quantum systems requires density matrix simulation where memory and computation scale as O(4^N). For N=4, rho is 16x16, permitting exact simulation of 420 sweep runs in minutes. 4 PCA features capture 60.04% of total variance across chest X-rays without data leakage."
    },
    {
      "q": "How was data leakage strictly prevented during preprocessing?",
      "a": "Both the PCA projection matrix and the MinMaxScaler feature transformation were fit STRICTLY on the 300 training samples. The test set was transformed using frozen parameters and clipped to [0, pi]."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why Ry angle encoding into [0, pi] rather than amplitude encoding?",
      "a": "Angle encoding requires constant circuit depth 1 (parallel single-qubit gates), minimizing gate exposure to noise before the ansatz begins. Amplitude encoding requires complex O(2^N) entangling circuits, introducing fatal errors on NISQ hardware. [0, pi] ensures a bijective mapping: 0 -> |0> and pi -> |1> without periodic wrapping."
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the physical difference between Depolarizing and Amplitude Damping noise?",
      "a": "Depolarizing noise is symmetric white noise that contracts the Bloch sphere isotropically toward I/2. Amplitude damping is asymmetric T1 energy relaxation that decays excited states |1> to ground state |0>, shifting expectation values toward +1."
    },
    {
      "q": "Does depolarizing noise flip the sign of the prediction or only shrink it?",
      "a": "It ONLY shrinks it! Tr(E(rho) Z0) = (1 - 4p/3) <Z0>. Since (1 - 4p/3) > 0 for all p <= 0.05, the sign of <Z0> never changes. At L=1, exactly 0.0 out of 100 test predictions flip across all seeds up to 5% noise."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does Amplitude Damping cause a catastrophic collapse in Recall?",
      "a": "In our readout scheme, <Z0> = -1 corresponds to Pneumonia (class 1). Amplitude damping decays states into |0> (<Z0> = +1, Normal). This forces negative expectation values across the zero threshold to positive values, systematically misdiagnosing Pneumonia patients as Normal and causing massive False Negatives."
    },
    {
      "q": "What happened with the initial Small MLP standard deviation of 15%?",
      "a": "At lr=0.05, Seed 46 suffered dying ReLUs and saturated biases, collapsing to ln(2) loss and predicting class 1 for 98% of points (52% accuracy). Calibrating lr=0.01 with Kaiming normal initialization and 300 epochs eliminated the collapse, lowering std from 15.19% down to 2.07% (84.40% +/- 2.07%)."
    },
    {
      "q": "Is the Linear vs Circular topology difference larger than seed-to-seed variance?",
      "a": "At depth L=2, the clean difference is exactly 0.00%, which is much smaller than the seed spread (+/- 4.12%). At L=2 under 5% noise, the difference (+0.6%) is also within noise. However, at L=3 under 5% Amplitude Damping, Linear holds a +12.4% advantage (78.8% vs 66.4%), which is larger than the seed spread."
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does reading out the LAST qubit eliminate the L=1 Linear gap?",
      "a": "In Linear topology, CNOTs cascade from wire 0 down to wire 3. Qubit 0 acts only as a control and receives no information from qubits 1, 2, or 3. Measuring the last qubit (wire 3) accesses the full accumulated entanglement cascade, causing Linear L=1 accuracy to jump from 61.60% to 77.80%, exactly matching Circular L=1!"
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the new crossover rate against Logistic Regression (84.80%)?",
      "a": "Clean VQC reaches 84.60% at L=3 Circular (within 0.20% of LogReg). Using the 1-baseline-std lower bound (B - 1s = 82.31%), Circular L=3 stays within this tolerance up to 5.00% Depolarizing noise and up to 2.00% Amplitude Damping noise."
    }
  ],
